+# Design Note — Resume Builder screen decomposition
+
+> Lightweight tier per `reviews/mobile/09_CODEX_CROSSCHECK_POLICY.md` §1.5. The POC's `ResumeBuilderScreen.js` is one 1962-LOC file with 25+ `useState`s, 30+ handlers and 16 render-* helpers; porting it verbatim violates our anti-mega-screen rule. The *structural* choice (how to split it) is non-trivial but reversible — full Design Options doc is overkill.
+
+**Date:** 2026-06-05
+**Author:** Claude Opus 4.7 (drafted for CEO + dev review)
+**Status:** SUPERSEDED-BY [`docs/design/2026-06-05-resume-builder-screen-decomposition.md`](../2026-06-05-resume-builder-screen-decomposition.md) — Codex r1 BLOCKED on tier mismatch (~1,630 LOC estimate exceeds the ≤ 1 engineer-day threshold for Note tier). Full Design Options doc written at the linked path. Do NOT consume this Note for the decision — its analysis is preserved here for history, with three corrections noted in the SUPERSEDED-BY doc: (i) `expo-router` version is `~4.0.21` not `^3.5.18`, (ii) Option B's "all sections in memory" claim is overstated, (iii) reversibility "≤4 hours" is too tight; revised estimate is 1 engineer-day including tests + Codex round. Added Option 4 (2-route + bottom sheet) per Codex r1's "Sixth option (probe C)" suggestion.
+**Tier:** Design Note — INVALID for this scope per Codex r1 BLOCK on tier mismatch.
+**Related phase:** Phase 1 — Resume Builder port (continues PR #1 `feat/resume-builder-port`)
+**Depends on:** Conflict 1 (auth-bootstrap) resolved per `[[careerbot-mobile-screen-port-conflict-auth-bootstrap]]`. Implementation cannot begin while that's open.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Context
+
+The POC `src/screens/resumeBuilder/ResumeBuilderScreen.js` is a single 1962-LOC file holding the entire builder UX: a "Your Resumes" landing list, the section grid for an open resume, every per-section editor (record / list-entry / skills), the AI Writer trigger, the template-applied callback, the save/sync state machine, and the upload placeholder. It owns ≥25 `useState` hooks, ≥30 in-body handler functions, and 16 nested `render*` helpers. None of the helpers are exported.
+
+This Design Note decides **how the same UX gets re-expressed as Expo Router routes + extracted hooks + section components** in production careerbot-mobile, before any port code is written. The decision matters because the POC's single-file shape would (a) breach our anti-mega-screen rule (no production file >800 LOC, no React component >400 LOC), (b) force every section-editor change to re-render the whole resume list, and (c) make any per-section unit test impossible without rendering the entire tree.
+
+Scope EXCLUDES `BuilderPreviewScreen.js` (199 LOC) and `TemplateSelectionScreen.js` (463 LOC) — both small enough to port as one route each in a follow-up commit.
+
+## 2. Acceptance criteria
+
+- [ ] No new file exceeds **400 LOC** (function components or hooks).
+- [ ] No new React component holds more than **6 useState/useReducer hooks directly**; everything else is consolidated into named custom hooks (`useResumeDraft`, `useSectionRouter`, `useSkillsEditor`).
+- [ ] All resume / template **server state goes through TanStack Query** (already ported in `src/features/resumes/{resumesApi,templatesApi}.ts`); no `useEffect(() => fetchAuthed(…))` patterns.
+- [ ] Each per-section editor (`RecordEditor`, `ListEditor`, `SkillsEditor`) is **independently renderable in tests** with a stub draft + onSave (no nested context required).
+- [ ] **Reversibility ≤ 4 hours**: the chosen route shape can be collapsed back into a single route by re-importing the extracted components and pasting the route bodies together — verified by writing the collapse plan as a comment in the index route.
+
+## 3. Options
+
+### Option A — Section-per-route (one Expo Router file per section)
+
+The 8 primary RESUME_SECTIONS + 6 EXTRA_SECTIONS each become their own route:
+
+```
+app/(tabs)/resume-builder/
+  index.tsx                  Your Resumes landing (list of saved resumes)
+  [resumeId]/index.tsx       Section grid + completeness ring for one resume
+  [resumeId]/personal.tsx    one route per section, type-safe params
+  [resumeId]/education.tsx
+  [resumeId]/experience.tsx
+  …  (14 section routes)
+```
+
+Each route handles its own form state via `useResumeDraft(resumeId, sectionKey)`. Navigation is push-based; the section grid is a `Link href={`/${resumeId}/education`}` tile.
+
+**Pros**
+- Native back-stack and deep-linkability (push notifications can deep-link "complete your skills" to `/<id>/skills`).
+- Smallest individual files (<200 LOC each section).
+- Per-section URL state — refresh / browser back are free.
+
+**Cons**
+- 14 new route files for what is conceptually one screen.
+- Optimistic-save UX cross-route is harder (need a draft-in-flight banner that survives route push).
+- Tab persistence: switching tabs mid-edit and back must re-hydrate route params, not just zustand state.
+
+**Evidence row** — Expo Router v3 docs: "Each file in the app directory is a route" (https://docs.expo.dev/router/introduction/, 2026-04). Confirms each section file ships as its own JS chunk lazily evaluated on navigation, which is the source of the "smaller bundle on initial mount" claim. POC's single-screen first-render at minified ~78 KB JS would split to ~6 KB index + ~5 KB per section under this option (measured by Metro `--analyze` on a JS-only equivalent in `automation/` 2026-05).
+
+### Option B — Single screen + extracted hooks + section components (one route, internal split)
+
+Keep one Expo Router route, but split the file:
+
+```
+app/(tabs)/resume-builder/
+  index.tsx                                ~150 LOC — landing + section-grid view dispatcher
+
+src/features/resumes/
+  components/
+    SectionGrid.tsx                        ~120 LOC — the 8+6 tile grid
+    RecordEditor.tsx                       ~180 LOC — record-kind sections (Personal, Summary, Hobbies)
+    ListEditor.tsx                         ~220 LOC — list-kind sections (Experience, Education, …)
+    SkillsEditor.tsx                       ~280 LOC — the categorised + custom skills UX
+    YourResumesLanding.tsx                 ~150 LOC — landing list of saved resumes + create button
+    UploadPlaceholder.tsx                  ~80 LOC
+  hooks/
+    useResumeDraft.ts                      ~180 LOC — values, draft, errors, sync, debounced PATCH
+    useSectionRouter.ts                    ~100 LOC — activeKey, mode, editIndex, openSection, closeToList
+    useSkillsEditor.ts                     ~140 LOC — pendingSkills, addCategorySkill, addCustomCategory, removeCustomSkill
+```
+
+The route file is a state-machine dispatcher: it reads from `useResumeDraft` and `useSectionRouter` and renders one of (landing | section-grid | RecordEditor | ListEditor | SkillsEditor).
+
+**Pros**
+- Easiest 1:1 with POC structure — every nested handler becomes a hook method. Minimises port risk.
+- One URL, one back button — no cross-route session restoration complexity.
+- Easier to add "section preview side-by-side" later (everything is in one render tree).
+
+**Cons**
+- Index route still ~150 LOC even after extraction (a state dispatcher is irreducible work).
+- No deep-linkable section URLs (push notifications must go through a query param, e.g. `/resume-builder?section=skills`, with route-side handling to open the editor).
+- All sections live in memory at once — for very long resumes this is more work per re-render than Option A.
+
+**Evidence row** — Independent measurement on the existing production code: `app/(auth)/signin.tsx` is 167 LOC with 4 useStates and one custom hook; it's the largest "screen as dispatcher" file we ship today and is comfortably under our 400-LOC rule. Same shape applied here yields ~150 LOC for the route file (manual count of dispatcher + 3 case-arms, no helpers).
+
+### Option C — Hybrid: 3 stable routes + modal section editor + extracted hooks/components
+
+Three real routes (one per phase of the UX), with the section-editor opened as a modal:
+
+```
+app/(tabs)/resume-builder/
+  index.tsx              Your Resumes landing (~150 LOC, uses YourResumesLanding component)
+  [resumeId]/
+    index.tsx            Section grid + completeness for one resume (~150 LOC, uses SectionGrid)
+    section/
+      [sectionKey].tsx   Modal route — record/list/skills editor (~180 LOC, dispatches to RecordEditor / ListEditor / SkillsEditor)
+```
+
+Same `src/features/resumes/components/*` + `hooks/*` split as Option B, but the editors are reached by `router.push('/${resumeId}/section/skills', { presentation: 'modal' })` instead of an internal state machine.
+
+**Pros**
+- Three routes match the three mental modes (browse resumes → browse sections → edit section) — back button + Android hardware-back do the right thing automatically.
+- Modal presentation makes "save / cancel" semantics obvious and matches platform conventions (iOS Settings, Android Material modals).
+- Section editor is deep-linkable for push notifications without query-param hacks.
+- File-size budget achievable: every file <250 LOC after extraction; each section component <300 LOC.
+
+**Cons**
+- Three routes means three places where draft state must be coordinated; without TanStack Query's optimistic update + zustand-backed draft hook, sections opened as modals would lose unsaved edits on dismiss.
+- The "completeness" ring in the section grid must read from the same draft hook the modal writes to, OR rely on cache invalidation on modal close.
+
+**Evidence row** — Expo Router modal recipe (https://docs.expo.dev/router/advanced/modals/, accessed 2026-06-05) and our own ADR-0006 (no native code edits, prefer JS-only navigation patterns): modal presentation requires no native code under expo-router ≥ 3.5, which is what we ship (per `careerbot-mobile/package.json:expo-router@^3.5.18`). Cross-route draft state via a zustand store is the pattern used in `src/features/auth/authStore.ts` and validated in 4 Codex rounds — no new architectural primitive needed.
+
+## 4. Decision
+
+**Chosen:** **Option C** — three stable routes (resumes-list → section-grid → section-editor modal) with extracted hooks/components.
+
+**Why** — Option C is the only one where the file-size budget (§2 criterion 1) is **demonstrably** achievable: each of the three route files plus each of the four extracted editor components stays under 300 LOC by manual function-LOC summing of the POC source (no file approaches 400). Option A's 14 routes overshoot the routing-state cost (§3 con 2-3) for a feature where deep links to specific sections are a "v1.x maybe" not a "v1 must"; Option B's single-route dispatcher still bears all the section render trees in memory simultaneously, which conflicts with §2 criterion 4 (independent renderability per test). The modal presentation in Option C also matches platform-native behaviour on save/cancel — evidence row references official Expo Router modal recipe, not vendor copy.
+
+## 5. Reversibility
+
+Worst case (wrong choice in 3 months): collapse three routes into one by re-importing `<YourResumesLanding>`, `<SectionGrid>`, `<RecordEditor | ListEditor | SkillsEditor>` into a single `app/(tabs)/resume-builder/index.tsx` and replacing `router.push` calls with `setActiveKey` state updates. Estimated touchpoints: 3 route files deleted (~600 LOC removed), 1 route file rewritten (~400 LOC added). No data migration (TanStack Query cache is route-agnostic). No user-visible disruption beyond the navigation animation changing from modal to inline (no re-auth, no re-onboarding). Calendar cost: 3–4 hours single-engineer including running the full test suite.
+
+The fast-collapse plan ships as a top-of-file comment in `app/(tabs)/resume-builder/index.tsx` so future-us doesn't have to rediscover it.
+
+## 6. Why not a full Design Options doc?
+
+This is the internal structure of one isolated feature module — no auth, payments, native code, contract surface, secrets, or any other gated path is touched. The reversibility cost is under one engineer-day per §5, and the worst case is "feature feels different", not "feature breaks". This fits the Design Note threshold per §1.5 cleanly.
+
+## 7. Sign-off
+
+| Persona | Verdict | Date |
+|---|---|---|
+| Codex (design-lens review) | TBD | |
+| Brother / CEO (one of) | TBD | |
+
+---
+
+## Pre-decision dependencies (must be resolved BEFORE implementation begins)
+
+1. **Conflict 1 — auth bootstrap** resolved per `[[careerbot-mobile-screen-port-conflict-auth-bootstrap]]`. All three routes will inherit the cold-start behaviour decided there.
+2. **PR #1 merged.** This Design Note's implementation will sit on top of `src/features/resumes/` + `src/components/resume/` shipped by PR #1.
+3. **Codex `ACCEPT-AS-IS` on this Design Note.** Per §1.5, code cannot begin until the design review passes.
